   \code{gradient_tolerance} \tab analytic GLM, \code{conditional_logit} \tab \code{0.001} \tab Largest projected gradient a winning start may have \cr
   \code{kernel_engine} \tab analytic GLM, \code{conditional_logit} \tab \code{"reference"} \tab \code{"stream"} computes kernel values and derivatives with less copying \cr
   \code{theta_bounds} \tab analytic GLM \tab \code{c(1e-4, 1e5)}; \code{c(1e-4, Inf)} for \code{joint_nb2} \tab Search bounds for NB2 theta \cr
   \code{theta_start} \tab analytic GLM \tab \code{NULL} \tab Starting NB2 theta \cr
   \code{coefficient_start} \tab analytic GLM, NB2 only \tab \code{"cold"} \tab \code{"warm"} reuses coefficients within a search \cr
   \code{worker_pool} \tab \code{joint_nb2} only \tab \code{NULL} \tab PSOCK cluster for running starts in parallel \cr
}


Each row of \code{starts} holds positive kernel scales in internal distance units
(map distance divided by \code{unit_conv}), followed by shapes for \code{"expow"}, in
model predictor order. Explicit starts replace the deterministic scale
starts, and \code{par}, if supplied, is added first. All independent starts are
kept. NB2 dispersion starts cycle through the initial theta, 0.3, 2, and 20
within the bounds. For \code{"profile_glm"}, theta bounds must be positive,
increasing, and at most 1e6. For \code{"joint_nb2"}, the upper bound must stay
infinite and the lower bound must be at least 1e-8. Each \code{"joint_nb2"}
kernel start is tried at the exact Poisson boundary and at an interior
dispersion, giving 12 fits with the default six kernel starts.

The \code{"stream"} kernel engine traverses the prepared matrices contiguously,
copies them less, and caches response-only NB2 terms. It keeps the same
starts, likelihood, and uncertainty checks. It uses extra temporary vectors
proportional to the number of observations and can speed up larger binned
inputs. For smooth weighted SQ predictors under \code{"conditional_logit"}, it
also computes values and scale derivatives in compiled code, reusing work
buffers across sites, with temporary storage proportional to the largest
retained site buffer.
}

